// The zipfile package: ZIP archives (PKWARE APPNOTE 6.3.10) read, written and extracted safely. Written
// in Cone over the deflate package (method 8, and the CRC-32) and fs (files and folders), with no C
// library. It is named zipfile, not zip, because a module called zip cannot exist: core has a public
// function 'zip' (the two-collection walk), and a module may not share a name with what core brings in
// (Error 1011), while Congo makes a package's module the package's name (Error 1130).
//
//   mod unpack;
//   import stdio use *;
//   import zipfile;
//
//   fn main() i32 {
//     // 'bytes' is the archive in memory (fs.readFile, or fs.mapFile for one too big to read whole)
//     match zipfile.Archive.open(bytes) {
//       case let Ok(ar) {
//         match ar.readNamed(bytes, "docs/readme.txt") {              // one entry, the others untouched
//           case let Ok(text) { ... }
//           case let Error(e) { printStr(e.reason.view()); }
//         }
//         imm opts = zipfile.ExtractOptions.safe();
//         ar.extractAll(bytes, "out", &opts);                         // every name checked first
//       }
//       case let Error(e) { printStr(e.reason.view()); return 1i32; }
//     }
//     0i32;
//   }
//
// 'examples/tour.cone' writes, lists, reads, streams and extracts; 'examples/bench.cone' times them.
//
// WHAT IS HERE
//
//   READING          the archive's bytes are the caller's, lent to each call (a value cannot hold a borrow)
//     Archive.open(data) Result[Archive, ZipError]     reads the end records and the central directory
//       count(), entry(i) &Entry, find(name) Option[usize], comment, prefix, zip64, entries
//       read(data, i), readNamed(data, name)           the whole entry, checked: Result[List[u8], ZipError]
//       reader(data, i) Result[EntryReader, ZipError]  the entry in bounded pieces
//       extractAll(data, dest, &opts), extractOne(data, i, dest, &opts)   to a folder, safely
//     EntryReader: pull(data, &mut out, max) Status (MoreOutput, Done, Failed), error(), name(), size(),
//       delivered(). Like an Inflater's, but the whole input is there, so NeedInput never happens.
//     Entry: name, rawName, comment, method, flags, crc, compressedSize, size, offset, madeBy,
//       externalAttrs, dosDate, dosTime, modified, modifiedExact; isDir(), isSymlink(), isEncrypted(),
//       unixMode(), methodName()
//   WRITING          entries are added whole; the bytes made come out of 'pull' in pieces of any size
//     ZipWriter.make(), add(name, data, &opts), addDir(name, &opts), addFile(name, path, &opts),
//       addTree(root, prefix, &opts), setComment(text), setZip64(always), finish(), pull(&mut out, max)
//       Status (NeedInput, MoreOutput, Done), bytes() Result[List[u8], ZipError] (finish and take all),
//       saveTo(path) (finish and write what has not been pulled), count(), isFinished()
//     EntryOptions: method, level, modified, mode, zip64, comment; plain(), stored(), deflated(level)
//   OPTIONS AND RESULTS
//     ExtractOptions { overwrite, maxTotal }, safe(); Extracted { files, folders, bytes }
//   FAILURE          ZipError { kind, offset, reason }, message(), copy(); ErrorKind: NotZip, Truncated,
//     Corrupt, Unsupported, Encrypted, BadChecksum, BadData, TooLarge, UnsafePath, SymlinkEntry, Exists,
//     Io, NotFound, BadArgument
//
// HOW AN ARCHIVE IS READ
//
//   From the end: the end-of-central-directory record is looked for over the last 65535 + 22 bytes, the
//   one whose comment runs exactly to the end winning (a comment can hold bytes that look like a
//   record), else the nearest that fits. If a field of it is saturated (0xFFFF, 0xFFFFFFFF) and the
//   zip64 locator is there, the zip64 end record is read, and so are the zip64 extra fields of the
//   central records (the fields whose 32-bit places are 0xFFFFFFFF, in the order the format says). Bytes
//   in front of the archive (a self-extractor) are found by comparing where the directory ends with
//   where its offset says it starts; every offset is then counted past them ('Archive.prefix').
//   Only the central directory is read to open; an entry's data is touched only when it is read.
//   An archive in several disks is refused (Unsupported).
//   The central directory is the authority for sizes, CRC-32 and method. An entry's local header must
//   agree with it on the method and the name (as Python's zipfile requires of the name; two readers
//   disagreeing about what an archive says is how archives are made to hide things), and locates the
//   data past its own name and extra field. Its sizes are not compared (a data descriptor may hold the
//   real ones). When flag bit 3 is set, the data descriptor after the data is read in any of its four
//   shapes (with or without the signature, 4 or 8 bytes a size) and must say what the central directory
//   says; one that cannot be read at all is not held against the entry.
//   Every read checks the entry's size and CRC-32 at its end, and bounds the output by the size the
//   central directory declares (a deflate stream that goes past it fails TooLarge before the memory is
//   used, whatever the stream's expansion). Every length and offset is checked against the archive's
//   size before it is used, in 64 bits, so a hostile archive can be refused but not run off the end;
//   tests/refuse.cone cuts an archive at every length and flips bits in two archives.
//   Encrypted entries (flag bit 0 or 6, or the AES method 99) and methods other than stored and
//   deflate are listed, and refused on reading with Encrypted or Unsupported and a reason that says so.
//
// NAMES AND TIMES
//
//   An entry's name is the stored bytes if they are valid UTF-8 (ASCII included, with or without flag bit
//   11), else the bytes read as code page 437, the format's default; 'rawName' keeps the bytes. The
//   Info-ZIP Unicode Path field is used when its CRC-32 matches the header's name. A '\' in a name is
//   kept (Windows PowerShell 5.1's Compress-Archive writes 'dir\file.txt'); 'find' tries the name with its
//   '/' written '\' when the exact name is not there, and extraction takes '\' for a separator.
//   Of two entries with one name, 'find' answers the later (as Python's zipfile does).
//   'modified' is seconds since 1970: exact UTC from the extended timestamp field when the entry has one
//   ('modifiedExact'), else the DOS date and time read as if they were UTC (they are local time of an
//   unrecorded zone, to two seconds, 1980 on; a zero or impossible field is the nearest real one).
//
// HOW AN ARCHIVE IS WRITTEN
//
//   Each entry is written when added, whole: local header, then data, with the sizes and CRC-32 known, so
//   no data descriptor is written and nothing is patched. 'pull' hands out what has been made and drops
//   it, so an archive of many files can be written to a file or socket holding one entry and the central
//   directory (about 60 bytes an entry) at a time. Left out: adding an entry's data in pieces (a
//   streaming Deflater cannot fall back to storing, and a local header written first cannot say whether
//   the sizes need zip64).
//   Method 8 uses deflate's levels 0 to 2; a deflated entry that does not come out smaller is stored
//   instead, as are folders and empty files. A name that is not ASCII is written as UTF-8 with flag
//   bit 11 (an entry comment too); the Unix mode goes in the high half of the external attributes with
//   the host system 3, and an extended timestamp field carries a time given in 'modified' exactly (the
//   DOS fields hold it to 2 seconds, clamped to 1980 through 2107). A name already added is refused
//   (BadArgument), as is an empty one, one over 65535 bytes, and any method but 0 and 8. A name
//   extraction would refuse is written all the same (the writer is how the tests make hostile archives).
//   zip64 is written where something needs it: an entry of 4 GiB or more, an offset of 4 GiB or more, 65535
//   entries or more, a directory of 4 GiB or more, and for an entry whose options say 'zip64' (its local
//   header and central record then carry a zip64 field). 'setZip64(true)' writes it everywhere, with
//   every field of the end record saturated, which is how the readers of zip64 are tested; the zip64
//   archives the tests make were also read back by Python's zipfile, Info-ZIP's unzip -t and Windows'
//   tar (bsdtar).
//
// EXTRACTING SAFELY (extract.cone has the whole rule)
//
//   An archive is data from someone else and an entry's name is a path it chooses ("zip slip"). So
//   every entry is checked before the first byte is written, and one that fails ends the extraction
//   with nothing made: a name that is empty, holds a NUL, is absolute ('/x', '\x'), starts with a drive
//   letter ('C:x'), or has a '..' component (any, even one that stays inside: 'a/../b' is refused) is
//   UnsafePath; a '\' is a separator, so '..\x' is a '..' component; a symbolic-link entry (Unix mode type
//   0120000) is SymlinkEntry and never made; an encrypted entry or an unread method is refused. On Windows a name with
//   a character it cannot hold (< > : " | ? * or a control), a trailing space or dot, or a device name
//   (CON, NUL, COM1 and so on) is refused too; other systems take those names. A file already there is
//   Exists unless 'overwrite'; a folder in a file's way always is; two entries for one file are Exists
//   unless 'overwrite' (the later wins). A folder on a name's way, or the file itself, that is a link
//   (a link the archive did not make) is refused, because it could lead anywhere: this is checked by
//   listing the folder, since fs has no lstat, so a link that leads nowhere is not seen. 'maxTotal'
//   bounds the sum of the declared sizes. An entry that fails half way (a bad CRC-32, data that does not
//   inflate) removes its file; the files before it stay. Files are written a megabyte at a time.
//   Not restored: permissions (the execute bit is lost), times, owners; fs can set none of them.
//
// SPEED. Informative, on a loaded 13th-gen Core i7-13700HX, release build, examples/bench.cone: 200
// files of 1 MB (three quarters text that deflates about 8 to 1, a quarter noise that does not):
// whole-entry reads about 150 MB/s of data, streaming reads in 64 KiB pulls about 120 MB/s,
// extraction to disk about 90 MB/s. deflate's own inflate runs at 200 to 400 MB/s; what this adds is a
// byte-at-a-time copy into the caller's list (a List has no bulk append) and the CRC-32. Not chased.
//
// NOT HERE: encryption (ZipCrypto, AES) and signatures; methods 9 (Deflate64), 12 (bzip2), 14 (LZMA),
// 93 (zstd), 95 (xz), 98 (PPMd); archives in several disks; zip64 extensible data and the
// central-directory encryption of strong encryption; entries written in pieces; setting permissions or
// times on extracted files; updating an archive in place (read it, write another).
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports:
//
//   zipfile.cone     this overview
//   types.cone       Status, ErrorKind, ZipError, Entry, ExtractOptions, Extracted, EntryOptions
//   bytes.cone       little-endian fields, names (UTF-8 check, code page 437), the DOS clock
//   archive.cone     Archive: the end records, zip64, the central directory
//   reader.cone      locating an entry's data, EntryReader, whole-entry reads, data descriptors
//   extract.cone     the safety rules and the writing of files
//   writer.cone      ZipWriter

mod zipfile;

import collections use *;
import deflate;
import fs;
import path use Path;
